-- ======================================================================
--  Immersive Quest Markers: no squad dot on your own companions
-- ======================================================================
--  A recruited companion keeps drawing the ordinary faction squad dot on the
--  PDA map and the HUD minimap -- the same mark every other stalker squad in the
--  Zone gets, riding along a metre behind your own actor arrow, saying nothing
--  you did not already know. This removes it.
--
--  WHY IT IS THE DOT AND NOT THE ART. Every other map change this mod makes is a
--  RETEXTURE (modxml_n_iqm_map_icons) or a HINT rewrite (iqm_spothint), because
--  those are the two things a map spot has that can be changed without deciding
--  who gets one. There is no third look that fixes this: the dot is not wrong, it
--  is redundant, and the only fix for redundant is absent.
--
--  WHERE THE DOT COMES FROM, which is not where you would first look. The class
--  method is defined twice. sim_squad_scripted.script:1192 (ZCP's copy, the
--  winner for that path) declares `sim_squad_scripted:show`, and then
--  sim_squad_warfare.script:341 -- BASE ANOMALY, no mod overrides it -- replaces
--  it wholesale at file-body scope with `sim_squad_scripted.sim_squad_scripted.show`.
--  Script bodies run in filename order, `sim_squad_warfare` sorts after
--  `sim_squad_scripted`, so the warfare copy is the one that runs. Reading only
--  ZCP's file will tell you the spot is added on `commander_id()`; the live copy
--  adds it on `self.id`. Both are handled below, because which one is live is a
--  load-order fact and not a promise.
--
--  Either copy reaches the same verdict for a companion: not a monster squad,
--  on the actor's level, not an enemy faction -- so a PDA 3.0 shows it, and a
--  PDA 2.0 shows it whenever the companion happens to share your community.
--
--  HOW IT HOOKS. The wrapper is installed on the CLASS TABLE reached through the
--  script namespace (`sim_squad_scripted.sim_squad_scripted`), which is precisely
--  the handle sim_squad_warfare itself uses to replace the method -- rung 2 of the
--  hook tree. No unlocalizer, and no whole-file override of a 1200-line class that
--  ZCP owns and half the simulation runs through.
--
--  THE ORIGINAL IS NOT CALLED for a companion squad, which is the one place this
--  differs from iqm_spothint's wrapper and is deliberate. There the original had to
--  run because its side effects (removing the old spot, the visible_for_map flag)
--  are load-bearing for NPCs we then leave alone. Here the original's entire job IS
--  the spot we are refusing, and letting it place one so we could remove it again
--  would add and drop a map location on every squad update. Everything it would have
--  left behind is cleaned up by strip() instead.
--
--  WHAT COUNTS AS A COMPANION is axr_companions' own registry, `companion_squads`,
--  which is what the simulation itself tests (sim_squad_scripted.script:120). It
--  covers G_FLAT's Individually Recruitable Companions too: that mod clones the
--  stalker out of his own squad into the player's companion squad, so the one
--  registry answers for both routes.
--
--  AND THE TEST IS `~= nil`, NOT TRUTHINESS. axr_companions' load_state writes
--  `companion_squads[id] = false` for every saved companion and lets the squad
--  object be re-attached later (axr_companions.script:150-156), so a genuine
--  companion reads `false` for the first moments after a save load. The game's own
--  code tests the same way (`if companion_squads[squad.id] == nil`); a truthiness
--  test here would put the dot back for exactly as long as nobody was looking.
--
--  SOFT DEPENDENCY THROUGHOUT. Without axr_companions there are no companions and
--  the wrapper is never installed; without the class there is nothing to wrap. Bare
--  Anomaly and a GAMMA with the companion system removed both no-op, the same shape
--  as iqm_spothint's map_change_spot_hint gate.
--
--  NOTHING HERE OUTLIVES THE MOD. map_add_object_spot leaves the location
--  non-serializable (level_script.cpp:443-450), so squad dots are rebuilt from
--  sim_squad_scripted:update on every load and no save carries one. Switching the
--  option off, or uninstalling, puts every dot back on the next squad update --
--  which for an online companion is the next second. apply_config does not wait
--  for it; see there.
-- ======================================================================

local on = false   -- the feature switch (its own option AND the mod's master one)

--- The wrapped original, bound in on_game_start. Nil until then -- and nil forever
--  if the class or the companion system is absent, which is what apply_config's
--  sweep tests before it touches anything.
local original = nil

--- Is this squad one of the player's companions?
--
--  Reads the registry live rather than caching it: a squad becomes and stops being
--  a companion through dialog, at moments this module has no callback for, and a
--  stale cache would keep a dismissed stalker's dot off the map indefinitely.
local function is_party(squad)
	local ac = axr_companions
	if not (ac and ac.companion_squads and squad and squad.id) then return false end
	return ac.companion_squads[squad.id] ~= nil
end

--- Take the squad's map spot back off, whichever of the two routes placed it.
--
--  `warfare_spot` is the warfare branch's bookkeeping and always sits on `self.id`.
--  `spot_section` is the ordinary branch's, and the two live copies of show()
--  disagree about which id it was added on -- warfare uses `self.id`, ZCP uses
--  `current_spot_id` (the commander). Asking the engine which one actually carries
--  it settles that at runtime instead of betting on a load order.
--
--  `current_spot_id` is deliberately NOT cleared. ZCP's show() re-enters itself once
--  when that field disagrees with the commander, and leaving it alone avoids a
--  pointless recursion; the field is meaningless while no spot is placed anyway.
local function strip(squad)
	if not (level and level.map_has_object_spot) then return end

	if squad.warfare_spot then
		if level.map_has_object_spot(squad.id, squad.warfare_spot) ~= 0 then
			level.map_remove_object_spot(squad.id, squad.warfare_spot)
		end
		squad.warfare_spot = nil
	end

	if squad.spot_section then
		local sec = squad.spot_section
		local owner = squad.current_spot_id
		if owner and level.map_has_object_spot(owner, sec) ~= 0 then
			level.map_remove_object_spot(owner, sec)
		elseif level.map_has_object_spot(squad.id, sec) ~= 0 then
			level.map_remove_object_spot(squad.id, sec)
		end
		squad.spot_section = nil
	end
end

--- The wrapper around sim_squad_scripted.show.
--
--  Guarded on our own half only, and for the same reason iqm_spothint guards its:
--  show() runs from sim_squad_scripted:update for every squad in the simulation, so
--  an error raised here would not be one broken dot but a stalled A-life tick. A
--  failed strip falls through to vanilla, which is the visible-but-harmless outcome.
local function show(self)
	if on then
		local ok, party = pcall(is_party, self)
		if ok and party then
			pcall(strip, self)
			return
		end
	end
	return original(self)
end

--- Pulled by iqm_core at the end of read_config, like every other module's.
--
--  Sweeps the registry on a CHANGE in either direction rather than waiting for each
--  squad's next update. The wait would be short -- refresh() runs from update() and
--  an online companion updates constantly -- but "short" is not "none", and a switch
--  that visibly lags reads as a broken one. The ON direction strips; the OFF
--  direction hands each companion to the original, which puts its dot straight back.
function apply_config()
	local C = iqm_core and iqm_core.config and iqm_core.config()
	local want = (C and C.enabled and C.map_hide_companions) and true or false
	local was = on
	on = want

	if was == on or not original then return end

	local ac = axr_companions
	if not (ac and ac.companion_squads) then return end
	for id, _ in pairs(ac.companion_squads) do
		-- The registry's value is `false` between a save load and the squad being
		-- re-attached, so the squad is resolved by id rather than read out of it.
		local squad = alife_object(id)
		if squad then
			if on then pcall(strip, squad) else pcall(original, squad) end
		end
	end
end

--- Install the wrapper.
--
--  IDEMPOTENT WITHOUT A FILE-LOCAL FLAG, which a local could not be: loading a save
--  re-reads .script files, so a local flag comes back false while the wrapper it was
--  guarding may still be installed, and the second install would wrap the wrapper.
--  The identity of the installed function is therefore kept ON THE NAMESPACE, which
--  survives exactly as long as the patch does.
--
--  The live method is adopted as the original whenever it is NOT the wrapper we last
--  installed -- so a newer upstream show() (sim_squad_warfare re-running its body,
--  or another mod patching after us) is picked up rather than shadowed by a stale
--  copy, and our own wrapper is never stacked on itself.
function on_game_start()
	local ns = sim_squad_scripted
	local cls = ns and ns.sim_squad_scripted
	if not (cls and cls.show and axr_companions) then return end

	if cls.show ~= ns.iqm_squadspot_wrapper then
		ns.iqm_squadspot_original = cls.show
	end
	original = ns.iqm_squadspot_original
	ns.iqm_squadspot_wrapper = show
	cls.show = show
end
